[0] Validating the exact sign routine of Q(sqrt3) against 80-digit Decimal evaluation
  6096 elements (incl. 96 Pell-type a^2-3b^2=1 near-zero cases up to a~1e7): mismatches = 0

[1] Rotation by pi/3 and the six sectors, generated exactly
  R: cos = 1/2, sin = sqrt3/2 > 0, det = 1; R^k = I for k in 1..6 only at k = [6]
  boundaries LO[j] = R^j(sqrt3/2,-1/2) (angle j*pi/3 - pi/6); arc LO[j]->LO[j+1] has cos 1/2, sin>0,
  i.e. length exactly pi/3; bisector of sector 0 is (1,0); dir(j) = R^j(1,0) lies strictly inside sector j.
  angular measure: each open sector = pi/3, total 2*pi; the 6 boundary directions are null.
  exact sample directions: 3124 unit vectors in Q(sqrt3)^2; 3112 strictly inside a sector,
  12 on a boundary (skipped: null set).  Every sample classified into exactly one sector or boundary.
  'e in sector j => e+pi/3 in sector j+1 (mod 6)' on all 3112 interior samples: True
  (exact proof for ALL e: R maps LO[j] -> LO[j+1] incl. LO[5] -> LO[6] = LO[0], and det R = 1 preserves
   the sign of cross(LO[j], e) and cross(e, LO[j+1]); so R(sector j) = sector j+1 exactly.)

